messages.content:
Consider a bank account where two threads try to deposit money simultaneously; without synchronization, read-modify-write instructions interleave and corrupt the balance. A mutex (mutual exclusion lock) is designed to protect a critical section so that only one thread can access it at a time. A semaphore is a synchronization variable initialized to an integer count that can control access to a finite pool of resources or coordinate thread execution.

### Plan
1. Create a shared counter incremented concurrently by two threads.
2. Demonstrate mutual exclusion using a POSIX mutex.
3. Demonstrate signaling and counting using a POSIX semaphore.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define THREAD_ITERATIONS 100000

int counter_mutex_protected = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

int counter_sem_protected = 0;
sem_t sem;

void *increment_mutex(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < THREAD_ITERATIONS; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        counter_mutex_protected++;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

void *increment_sem(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < THREAD_ITERATIONS; i++) {
        sem_wait(&sem);
        counter_sem_protected++;
        sem_post(&sem);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL);
    sem_init(&sem, 0, 1);

    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, increment_mutex, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, increment_mutex, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, increment_sem, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, increment_sem, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("Mutex protected counter: %d\n", counter_mutex_protected);
    printf("Semaphore protected counter: %d\n", counter_sem_protected);

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock);
    sem_destroy(&sem);
    return 0;
}
```

### Correctness Analysis
- **Mutual Exclusion:** Both `pthread_mutex_lock` and `sem_wait` ensure that only one thread executes the increment operation on the shared counter at any given moment.
- **Progress & Deadlock:** Locks and semaphores are released in the same execution paths, preventing deadlocks.
- **No Lost Wakeups:** All critical sections complete without dropping updates.

### Compilation and Testing
```bash
gcc -Wall -pthread prog.c
gcc -fsanitize=thread -g -pthread prog.c
```

### Plan
1. Define a shared integer variable accessed concurrently by two threads.
2. Without synchronization, concurrent updates create a race condition where writes overwrite one another [1].
3. Establish an entry section using `pthread_mutex_lock()` and an exit section using `pthread_mutex_unlock()` around the shared update (the critical section) to enforce mutual exclusion [1].
4. Join both threads in `main` to confirm the shared counter matches the deterministic expected total.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define ITERS 10000

static int counter = 0;
static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *worker(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < ITERS; i++) {
        /* Entry section */
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

        /* Critical section */
        counter++;

        /* Exit section */
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;

    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, worker, NULL);

    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("Final counter: %d (expected: %d)\n", counter, 2 * ITERS);
    return 0;
}
```

### Correctness
- **Mutual exclusion**: `pthread_mutex_lock(&lock)` guarantees that at most one thread executes inside the critical section at any given time [1]. If thread A holds `lock`, thread B blocks at the entry section until thread A enters the exit section via `pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock)`.
- **Progress**: When no thread resides in the critical section and both threads contend for the lock, selection cannot be postponed indefinitely; one thread immediately acquires the mutex.
- **No deadlock**: Only a single lock is utilized, acquired and released in strict succession with no circular lock dependencies [1].
- **No lost wakeups**: The implementation relies purely on mutual exclusion; because no condition variables or sleep-queues are involved [2], no wakeup events exist to be missed.

### Compilation and Testing
Compile and run the program:
```bash
gcc -Wall -pthread prog.c
./a.out
```

To test for data races, compile with ThreadSanitizer:
```bash
gcc -fsanitize=thread -g -pthread prog.c
./a.out
```
ThreadSanitizer outputs zero warnings. If you comment out the `pthread_mutex_lock` and `pthread_mutex_unlock` calls, ThreadSanitizer will immediately report a data race on `counter`.

If two threads increment `counter` without the mutex, what specific sequence of machine instructions (load, modify, store) causes one increment to be lost?

Plan: Serialize sorted insertion and close the submission phase with joins before printing.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <assert.h>
typedef struct Node { int priority; struct Node *next; } Node;
static pthread_mutex_t m=PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static Node *head;
static int priority[4]={2,4,1,3},printed[4];
static void *submit(void *p) {
    Node *n=malloc(sizeof *n); assert(n); n->priority=*(int *)p;
    assert((pthread_mutex_lock(&m)) == 0); Node **link=&head;
    while(*link && (*link)->priority>n->priority) link=&(*link)->next;
    n->next=*link; *link=n; assert((pthread_mutex_unlock(&m)) == 0); return NULL;
}
static void *printer(void *p) {
    (void)p;
    for(int i=0;i<4;i++) {
        assert((pthread_mutex_lock(&m)) == 0); Node *n=head; assert(n); head=n->next;
        assert((pthread_mutex_unlock(&m)) == 0); printed[i]=n->priority; free(n);
    } return NULL;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_t s[4],p; for(int i=0;i<4;i++) assert((pthread_create(&s[i],NULL,submit,&priority[i])) == 0);
    for(int i=0;i<4;i++) assert((pthread_join(s[i],NULL)) == 0);
    assert((pthread_create(&p,NULL,printer,NULL)) == 0); assert((pthread_join(p,NULL)) == 0);
    for(int i=0;i<4;i++) assert(printed[i]==4-i);
    assert(!head); assert((pthread_mutex_destroy(&m)) == 0); return 0;
}
```

Why it is correct: Insertion preserves list order under m. The printer detaches each node before freeing it, and submitters have already finished, so lifetime and final priority order are well-defined. The batch barrier avoids claiming future arrivals can be printed in advance.

