Verdict: bug found

Line 26: `ck(pthread_detach(t));`
Line 27: `free(job);`

Detaching only changes thread-resource reclamation. It does not keep the pointed-to job alive or wait for the worker to finish.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | worker created with heap job |  | worker may not have started |
| 2 |  | main detaches worker | detachment establishes no completion wait |
| 3 |  | main frees job | storage released |
| 4 | worker writes *job |  | possible use-after-free |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -23,7 +23,7 @@
 int main(void) {
     pthread_t t; int *job=malloc(sizeof *job); assert(job); *job=0;
     ck(pthread_create(&t,NULL,worker,job));
-    ck(pthread_detach(t));
+    ck(pthread_join(t,NULL));
     free(job);
     return 0;
 }
```

Retain a joinable thread and join before free. The job has no user after the join; a detached design would instead need an explicit ownership-transfer or completion protocol.

Plan: Pass one baton between two entry sections.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <assert.h>
#include <string.h>
static sem_t go[2];
static int ids[2]={0,1},used;
static char text[9];
static void *player(void *p) {
    int id=*(int *)p;
    for(int turn=0;turn<4;turn++) {
        assert(sem_wait(&(go[id])) == 0); assert(used%2==id); text[used++]=(char)('A'+id); assert(sem_post(&(go[1-id])) == 0);
    } return NULL;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_t t[2]; assert((sem_init(&go[0],0,1)) != -1); assert((sem_init(&go[1],0,0)) != -1);
    for(int i=0;i<2;i++) assert((pthread_create(&t[i],NULL,player,&ids[i])) == 0);
    for(int i=0;i<2;i++) assert((pthread_join(t[i],NULL)) == 0);
    text[used]='\0'; assert(strcmp(text,"ABABABAB")==0); puts(text);
    assert((sem_destroy(&go[0])) != -1); assert((sem_destroy(&go[1])) != -1); return 0;
}
```

Why it is correct: A starts with the only permit; each player publishes its append before posting the peer. This handoff provides both exclusion and memory ordering for text and used. Equal finite turn counts leave no unmatched final wait.

