Consider two people attempting to withdraw money from the same ATM account at the exact same millisecond; if both read a balance of $100, subtract $50, and write back $50, one transaction is completely lost. When multiple threads access shared memory without synchronization, instructions interleave arbitrarily, leading to a race condition [2].

### Plan
1. Define a global shared counter variable initialized to zero [2].
2. Spawn two worker threads that each loop 10 million times, incrementing the counter without locks [2].
3. Use a semaphore to fix the race condition and ensure correct final values.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define LOOPS 1000000

long shared_counter = 0;
sem_t lock;

void *worker(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < LOOPS; i++) {
        sem_wait(&lock);
        shared_counter++;
        sem_post(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    sem_init(&lock, 0, 1);

    printf("Initial counter: %ld\n", shared_counter);

    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, worker, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, worker, NULL);

    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);

    printf("Final counter: %ld (Expected: %d)\n", shared_counter, LOOPS * 2);

    sem_destroy(&lock);
    return 0;
}
```

### Correctness Analysis
- **Mutual Exclusion:** Wrapping `shared_counter++` with `sem_wait` and `sem_post` guarantees that the read-modify-write sequence executes as an atomic critical section.
- **Progress & Deadlock:** Since there is only a single semaphore protecting a single variable, no circular wait can occur, ensuring deadlock-free progress.
- **No Lost Updates:** Every increment performed by either thread is safely preserved.

### Compilation and Testing
```bash
gcc -Wall -pthread prog.c
gcc -fsanitize=thread -g -pthread prog.c
```

Can you trace what final counter value you would expect if the semaphore calls were omitted entirely?

Verdict: bug found

Line 23: `while(busy) {}`
Line 24: `busy=1;`
Line 26: `busy=0;`

Separate ordinary test and set operations do not form an atomic entry section. busy itself races and both contenders can reach conflicting owner accesses.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | read busy -> 0 |  | gate appears free |
| 2 |  | read busy -> 0 | both contenders passed check |
| 3 | store busy=1 |  | A thinks it owns gate |
| 4 |  | store busy=1 | B also thinks it owns gate |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -20,10 +20,9 @@
 static unsigned char busy;
 static int owner=-1;
 static void claim(int id) {
-    while(busy) {}
-    busy=1;
+    while(__atomic_test_and_set(&busy,__ATOMIC_ACQUIRE)) {}
     if(owner==-1) owner=id;
-    busy=0;
+    __atomic_clear(&busy,__ATOMIC_RELEASE);
 }
 static void *a(void *p) { (void)p; claim(0); return NULL; }
 static void *b(void *p) { (void)p; claim(1); return NULL; }
```

Atomic test-and-set grants entry to one observer of the clear byte, and release-clear hands off protected state. This provides exclusion, not FIFO fairness.

Verdict: bug found

Line 24: `ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&outbox)); return NULL;`

The exit section names a different mutex. It neither owns outbox nor releases inbox; the invalid unlock has no portable default-type semantics.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | lock(inbox) succeeds |  | inbox=A; outbox free |
| 2 | increment removed |  | A still owns inbox |
| 3 | unlock(outbox) |  | wrong object; inbox not released |
| 4 |  | lock(inbox) attempts | inbox remains unavailable; prior unlock invalid |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@
 static int removed;
 static void *a(void *p) {
     (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&inbox)); ++removed;
-    ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&outbox)); return NULL;
+    ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&inbox)); return NULL;
 }
 static void *b(void *p) { return a(p); }
 int main(void) {
```

Unlocking inbox matches the actual acquisition and lets the next remover enter. The unrelated outbox remains untouched until safe destruction.

Verdict: bug found

Line 28: `printf("result=%d\n",result);`

Producer completion proves input publication, not consumer completion. Main may read result before or during the consumer’s write.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | producer signals full |  | packet published |
| 2 |  | main joins producer | consumer can still be pending |
| 3 |  | main reads result | final transformation not ordered complete |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -25,6 +25,8 @@
     pthread_t p,c; SO(sem_init(&full,0,0));
     ck(pthread_create(&p,NULL,producer,NULL)); ck(pthread_create(&c,NULL,consumer,NULL));
     ck(pthread_join(p,NULL));
+    ck(pthread_join(c,NULL));
     printf("result=%d\n",result);
+    SO(sem_destroy(&full));
     return 0;
 }
```

Join the consumer before reading its output and before destroying the event semaphore. Distinct pipeline stages need their own completion edges.

Verdict: bug found

Line 14: `(void)arg; pthread_mutex_lock(&r); pthread_mutex_lock(&l);`

The two entry sections acquire the same exclusive resources in opposite orders [2].

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | lock(l) succeeds |  | l=A, r=free |
| 2 |  | lock(r) succeeds | l=A, r=B |
| 3 | lock(r) blocks |  | A waits for B |
| 4 |  | lock(l) blocks | B waits for A; circular wait |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -11,8 +11,8 @@
     result += 4; pthread_mutex_unlock(&r); pthread_mutex_unlock(&l); return NULL;
 }
 static void *b_worker(void *arg) {
-    (void)arg; pthread_mutex_lock(&r); pthread_mutex_lock(&l);
-    result += 4; pthread_mutex_unlock(&l); pthread_mutex_unlock(&r); return NULL;
+    (void)arg; pthread_mutex_lock(&l); pthread_mutex_lock(&r);
+    result += 4; pthread_mutex_unlock(&r); pthread_mutex_unlock(&l); return NULL;
 }
 int main(void) {
     pthread_t a,b; pthread_create(&a,NULL,a_worker,NULL); pthread_create(&b,NULL,b_worker,NULL);
```

Both entry sections now acquire l then r and exit in reverse order. Increasing acquisition order removes circular wait while still protecting result.

Plan: Each worker publishes its own completion before waiting for the peer.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <assert.h>
static sem_t prepared[2];
static int ids[2]={0,1},value[2],observed[2];
static void *worker(void *p) {
    int id=*(int *)p; value[id]=id==0?11:23;
    assert(sem_post(&(prepared[id])) == 0); assert(sem_wait(&(prepared[1-id])) == 0); observed[id]=value[1-id]; return NULL;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_t t[2]; assert((sem_init(&prepared[0],0,0)) != -1); assert((sem_init(&prepared[1],0,0)) != -1);
    for(int i=0;i<2;i++) assert((pthread_create(&t[i],NULL,worker,&ids[i])) == 0);
    for(int i=0;i<2;i++) assert((pthread_join(t[i],NULL)) == 0);
    assert(observed[0]==23 && observed[1]==11);
    assert((sem_destroy(&prepared[0])) != -1); assert((sem_destroy(&prepared[1])) != -1); return 0;
}
```

Why it is correct: Both wait/signal (P/V) sequences signal first, so initial zero counts cannot trap both before any post. Each array element has one writer and a peer reader ordered by the corresponding semaphore; joins order main’s checks.

